---
/**
 * foodcraft / GalleryBM — "Tahanan Cafe"'s room, in photographs.
 *
 * ROLE. The only section on the page that is nothing but the owner's own
 * pictures. A centred head — the underlined walnut eyebrow over one display
 * headline carrying the template's italic walnut emphasis — sitting above the
 * design's five-slot mosaic: a tall lead print spanning both rows beside four
 * smaller frames, all on the plain white ground.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="GALLERY" in
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   head           text-align:center; margin-bottom:34px
 *   eyebrow        10px / .34em caps, #7A4E2D, 700, on a 1px #E0D3C6 rule with
 *                  9px under it                                       → .kick
 *   headline       Libre Caslon Text 400, clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px), lh 1.14,
 *                  margin `.26em 0 0`, #241B14, and — like every other
 *                  headline in this design — NO max-width of its own
 *   emphasis       `Inside <em>…</em>`, italic #7A4E2D            → .h2 em
 *   grid           minmax(0,2fr) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr)
 *   rows           196px 196px                     gap 14px
 *   tile           #F7F2EC ground, 2px radius, overflow hidden    → .plate
 *   lead tile      grid-row: span 2
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE, and in fact no colour at all: every
 * colour the design names already has a wrapper token holding the same value
 * (#7A4E2D is --accent, #241B14 is --ink, #E0D3C6 is --line-2, #F7F2EC is
 * --well, 2px is --r) and every one of them arrives on a shared primitive.
 * This file states GEOMETRY only.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 *    group id 'gallery' — nothing else in that group exists) ───────────────────
 *   gallery.tag                        → the underlined walnut eyebrow (.kick)
 *   gallery.headline                   → the display h2, set:html
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.image   → data-image-field, one per mosaic frame
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.caption → the optional opaque ink caption pill
 * That is the whole list. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook no
 * sidebar input owns, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD. The design's slot placeholders — "The room",
 * "Counter", "Pastry case", "A plate", "Corner table" — are the DESIGNER
 * filling their own template. They are captions for photographs nobody has
 * looked at, so they are neither hard-coded nor invented as caption defaults:
 * an uncaptioned photo is simply a photo. The same goes for the head; the
 * design's own "Inside <em>{{ cafeName }}</em>" is the designer's copy, and an
 * owner who typed no headline gets no headline ELEMENT. Every guard sits
 * OUTSIDE its tag, so there is never an eyebrow hanging over nothing, never an
 * empty caption pill on a photo, and never a head block with nothing in it.
 *
 * ── THE SECTION GATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The section renders only when at least one row carries an IMAGE — the BODY
 * content, never the tag or the headline. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets
 * gallery.tag ('Inside') and gallery.headline ('A look at our work') on EVERY
 * site, so a gate counting those would ship an eyebrow and a display headline
 * over an empty grid. Once photos exist the tag and headline render (still
 * individually guarded) — refusing them would strand two live sidebar inputs.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, not authored ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * ROW INDEXES. Rows with no image are dropped, and the ORIGINAL array index
 * travels with the survivor as `.i`. The editor writes the data-field path
 * verbatim into the draft, so a re-indexed path would patch a different row:
 * clearing row 2's photo would misdirect row 3's caption edits into row 2.
 * Nothing here is numbered on screen, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * THE COUNT LADDER. The design hard-codes five slots; content does not.
 * derive-content-defaults hands over up to six photos and an owner may upload
 * one or eleven, so the design's five-up is the TOP of a ladder rather than the
 * only case, each tier drawn for its own count so none can open a hole:
 *   1   one full-width print, still two rows tall
 *   2   two full-height columns on the design's lopsided 2fr / 1fr
 *   3   the tall lead plus two stacked beside it
 *   4   the design's three tracks, the fourth frame taking both narrow ones
 *   5   the design's own grid, exactly
 *   6+  extras flow into implicit 196px rows and the LAST frame stretches over
 *       whatever tracks its row left empty, so the mosaic never ends on a
 *       ragged half-row. The stretch is computed PER BREAKPOINT — a three-track
 *       row and a two-track row run out at different counts — and is reset
 *       entirely at one column.
 *
 * ── DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE THINK WERE OVERSIGHTS ────────────────
 * · THE SECTION STATES NO VERTICAL PADDING. The design writes `padding:84px
 *   30px 0` on every section and lets the NEXT section's top padding be the
 *   whole gap. PageBM instead declares one SHARED, symmetric rhythm —
 *   `section { padding: clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0 }`, capped at the design's own
 *   84px — and that wrapper rule is the contract all fifteen sections in this
 *   variant sit on. Restating a bare-bottom here would make this the one
 *   section that butts into the next. The horizontal 30px is likewise not
 *   restated: .wrap is `min(1160px, 92vw)`, which is the design's 1160px cap
 *   and its side air in one declaration.
 * · THE 14px GAP IS DELIBERATE, and it is the one place in this design that
 *   has one. The family signature is grids drawn with BORDERS rather than gaps
 *   (HOW-IT-WORKS, the menu ledger, the FAQ all rule their cells) — but the
 *   GALLERY element specifies `gap:14px` on a gridful of clipped photo plates,
 *   where a hairline between two images would read as a seam. The design says
 *   gap; this file writes gap. It is not a leftover from a ruled sibling.
 * · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER. This section renders no link, no button
 *   and no numeral, and the 'gallery' schema group declares no href field at
 *   all — so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS would have nothing to remap and would
 *   ship as dead frontmatter. Omitted rather than carried unused. The section's
 *   own id="gallery" IS one of the page's five anchors, and is the target the
 *   header and footer link AT.
 * · THE CAPTION PILL IS NOT IN THE DESIGN, and that is not a liberty. The
 *   GALLERY element draws five bare tiles; its `placeholder="The room"` /
 *   "Counter" / "Pastry case" strings are the design tool's SLOT LABELS, shown
 *   in the editor and never rendered. But genericContentSchema declares
 *   `gallery.items.*.caption` as a live sidebar input, and PageBM ships a .cap
 *   primitive for exactly it — so the pill exists, guarded, or that input would
 *   be wired to nothing. No photo gets one unless the owner typed one, which is
 *   why the default rendering is the design's: bare tiles.
 * · The design's 34px down to the mosaic sits on the HEAD BLOCK, not on the
 *   h2's own margin-bottom. With both strings present it renders identically
 *   (the h2 keeps the design's .26em above and adds nothing below); with the
 *   headline cleared, an eyebrow-only head still clears the photos instead of
 *   gluing them to its underside.
 * · .h2 max-width is released to none. The wrapper caps every headline at 24ch,
 *   which is right for the design's left-aligned heads — but this head is
 *   CENTRED, and a 24ch block with `margin:… 0 0` would hug the left edge with
 *   its text centred inside a narrow column. The design gives this h2 no
 *   max-width, so neither does this file.
 * · min-height is zeroed on the frames. The wrapper floors .plate at 180px,
 *   which would fight the design's fixed 196px rows the moment they shrink.
 * · THE ITALIC WALNUT EMPHASIS comes from the WRAPPER's global `.h2 em` rule,
 *   not from here. set:html builds that <em> after Astro has scoped this
 *   stylesheet, so no scoped selector could reach it — which is exactly why
 *   nothing in this file needs :global(): the wrapper already owns it. The
 *   eyebrow's underline is likewise the shared .kick primitive and is NOT
 *   restated, because restating it is how a section loses the design's single
 *   most recognisable mark.
 * · THE HOVER LIFT is the only thing here the static design file could not
 *   express. The wrapper ships `transition: transform .5s ease` on .ph, which
 *   is dead weight unless a section moves it, and a photo mosaic is where it
 *   belongs. It is a 1.03 scale inside an already-clipped plate — the frame
 *   itself never moves, so no hairline or corner shifts — and reduced-motion
 *   cancels it.
 * · An apostrophe in an upload path would close the `url('…')` and blank the
 *   tile, so the path is percent-escaped FOR CSS ONLY. The raw value still
 *   travels into data-image-field, which is what the editor reads back.
 * · THE ROW HEIGHT GROWS at the one-column breakpoint (≤520 floors higher than
 *   ≤900 does), and that is intended, not the clamp inversion an audit found on
 *   the previous template. It is a BOX, not type: at ≤900 a frame is one of two
 *   ~234px columns and 148px keeps it near the design's 1.5:1; at ≤520 the same
 *   frame is the full ~478px width, and holding 148px would print a letterbox.
 *   Within each query the clamp is monotonic, and at the 900 boundary the cap
 *   (196px) meets the base row exactly, so nothing jumps there. No type size is
 *   restated in this file at any width — the wrapper owns the 760/520 pass.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A tile is announced only when the owner gave it words. With a caption the
 * photo layer is role="img" named by THAT CAPTION — the owner's own words — and
 * the caption is also the visible pill. With no caption the layer is HIDDEN
 * from the tree rather than announced as "image"; inventing "Cafe photo" would
 * publish a description of something nobody described. The two attributes are
 * mutually exclusive and BOTH conditional, so no literal aria-hidden ever sits
 * beside a conditional one.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const gallery = (content && content.gallery) || {};
const tag = typeof gallery.tag === 'string' ? gallery.tag.trim() : '';
const headline = typeof gallery.headline === 'string' ? gallery.headline.trim() : '';

type Tile = { image?: string; caption?: string };
// The legacy bare-array form lives at content.gallery ITSELF — not the
// `gallery` alias above, which is the object form's home. Both shapes reach
// production, so both are read.
const raw: Tile[] = Array.isArray(gallery.items)
  ? gallery.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.gallery) ? (content.gallery as Tile[]) : []);

const tiles = raw
  .map((t, i) => ({
    image: (t && typeof t.image === 'string' ? t.image : '').trim(),
    caption: (t && typeof t.caption === 'string' ? t.caption : '').trim(),
    i,
  }))
  .filter((t) => Boolean(t.image));

const n = tiles.length;

// CSS-only escape; the raw value still goes into data-image-field.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

/* Row completion, computed per breakpoint (see THE COUNT LADDER above).
   THREE tracks: the design's five fill rows 1–2 exactly, so extras open a fresh
   three-wide row — a remainder of 1 stretches the trailing frame over all three
   tracks, a remainder of 2 over two. Four photos is its own case, the fourth
   frame taking both narrow tracks.
   TWO tracks (≤900): the lead still spans both rows, frames 2 and 3 finish the
   second column, and the extras run two per row — so an even count leaves the
   trailing frame alone and it stretches across the pair. */
const rem3 = n > 5 ? (n - 5) % 3 : 0;
const wideDesk = n === 4 ? 2 : (rem3 === 1 ? 3 : (rem3 === 2 ? 2 : 0));
const wideMid = (n >= 4 && (n - 3) % 2 === 1) ? 2 : 0;
---
{n > 0 && (
  <section class="tcg" id="gallery">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="tcg-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="gallery.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 tcg-h2" data-field="gallery.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}

      <div
        class:list={[
          'tcg-mosaic',
          'reveal',
          n === 1 && 'tcg-x1',
          n === 2 && 'tcg-x2',
          n === 3 && 'tcg-x3',
        ]}
        data-delay="1"
      >
        {tiles.map((t, pos) => (
          <figure
            class:list={[
              'plate',
              'tcg-frame',
              pos === n - 1 && wideDesk === 3 && 'tcg-w3',
              pos === n - 1 && wideDesk === 2 && 'tcg-w2',
              pos === n - 1 && wideMid === 2 && 'tcg-wm2',
            ]}
          >
            <div
              class="ph"
              role={t.caption ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={t.caption || undefined}
              aria-hidden={t.caption ? undefined : 'true'}
              data-image-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.image`}
              style={`background-image:url('${cssUrl(t.image)}');`}
            ></div>
            {t.caption && (
              <figcaption class="cap" data-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.caption`}>{t.caption}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* .tcg carries NO rule on purpose. PageBM's shared `section` rhythm already
     states the design's 84px (capped there, symmetric by contract) and .wrap
     already states its 1160px / 92vw. The class stays in the markup as this
     section's root hook. Read the frontmatter before adding a bare bottom here:
     a padding-bottom:0 would make this the one section that butts into the
     next, because every sibling in the variant leans on the wrapper's rhythm.

     The design's centred head, and its 34px down to the mosaic carried by the
     BLOCK so an eyebrow with no headline still clears the photos. */
  .tcg-head {
    text-align: center;
    margin: 0 0 34px;
  }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps on .34em of tracking, so a
     single unbroken word (a pasted URL) is far wider than its character count
     suggests. Stated at EVERY width, not only in the 520 query: the wrapper's
     `body { overflow-x: hidden }` CLIPS an overflowing eyebrow rather than
     scrolling to it, so on a 900px viewport the tail of it would simply be
     gone. Only the wrapping is touched — restating .kick's font-size would
     cancel the wrapper's 520 pass, and restating its border-bottom would risk
     the signature underline outright. Matches WhyBM / HowBM / TestimonialsBM,
     which all state this unconditionally. */
  .tcg-head .kick {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* `.26em 0 0` verbatim; nothing below it (the block owns that). The wrapper's
     24ch cap is released because this head is centred — see the frontmatter. */
  .tcg-h2 {
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* ── THE MOSAIC ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The design's tracks verbatim: 2fr / 1fr / 1fr, two fixed 196px rows, a 14px
     gap, and the lead print spanning both rows. --row is one knob so the
     breakpoints move the row height without restating the grid.

     minmax(0,…) on every track, never a bare fr: a background-image tile has no
     intrinsic width, but a caption pill does, and one long caption in a bare
     1fr track would push the whole page sideways on a phone. */
  .tcg-mosaic {
    --row: 196px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: var(--row) var(--row);
    grid-auto-rows: var(--row);
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame:first-child { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Ground (--well), the 2px corner and the clip all arrive with .plate, which
     is exactly what the design specifies per tile — so only geometry is set
     here. The wrapper floors .plate at 180px, which would fight the fixed rows,
     so that floor is released. */
  .tcg-frame {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The one thing a static design file cannot state. See the frontmatter. */
  .tcg-frame:hover .ph { transform: scale(1.03); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .tcg-frame:hover .ph { transform: none; }
  }

  /* ── THE COUNT LADDER. Each tier restates the tracks for its own count so no
        tier can leave a hole. Two compounds plus Astro's scope attribute, so
        each out-specifies the base grid above AND the collapse rules below —
        wherever a tier is already narrow enough to survive the collapse. */
  .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x2,
  .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x2 .tcg-frame { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Row completion on the trailing frame (see the frontmatter). */
  .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-w3 { grid-column: span 3; }
  .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-w2 { grid-column: span 2; }

  /* ── 900px · the three-track mosaic collapses to two. The lead keeps its
        two-row span, which is what makes 3, 5, 7 … photos land on complete rows
        here while the even counts stretch their last frame. .tcg-x1 is
        deliberately NOT restated — it already out-specifies the base, so a lone
        photo keeps the full width instead of shrinking to a half tile beside an
        empty track — and .tcg-x2 / .tcg-x3 keep the design's lopsided 2fr / 1fr
        for the same reason. The 196px cap meets the base row exactly at this
        boundary, so nothing jumps as it is crossed. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tcg-mosaic {
      --row: clamp(148px, 23vw, 196px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-w3,
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-w2 { grid-column: auto; }
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-wm2 { grid-column: span 2; }
  }

  /* ── 520px · one column. A ~150px-wide tile is too small to read as a room,
        so the mosaic becomes a stack, every span is reset so nothing reaches
        for a track that is not there, and the row grows because each frame is
        now the full width (see the frontmatter — this is a box, not type).
        Nothing may push the page sideways at this width: the track is
        minmax(0,…), the frames cannot exceed it, the wrapper already breaks a
        long caption inside its pill, and the eyebrow's own break rule (stated
        at every width, above) keeps a pasted URL from running off the head. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .tcg-mosaic {
      --row: clamp(168px, 55vw, 256px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x2,
    .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame,
    .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x2 .tcg-frame,
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame:first-child { grid-row: auto; }
    .tcg-mosaic .tcg-frame.tcg-wm2 { grid-column: auto; }
    /* …except the solo photo, which stays a two-row print so it still reads as
       the lead image it is and not a stray thumbnail. Out-specifies the reset
       above on compound count, so it does not depend on source order. */
    .tcg-mosaic.tcg-x1 .tcg-frame:first-child { grid-row: span 2; }
  }
</style>
